    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 7th prioritizing aggressive baserunning and high-upside matchups. **Pete Crow-Armstrong** headlines our daily efficiency rankings with a robust 80.98, as he benefits from a favorable 4.77 xERA matchup against Lowder and a distinct boost from projected wind conditions blowing out, combined with his elite sprint speed potential. **Junior Caminero** remains a cornerstone of the offense at a 69.27 score; while his platoon disadvantage against the lefty Bennett is noted, his high base projection and prime cleanup-adjacent role at #3 ensure he remains a locked-in daily starter. **Trea Turner** rounds out the elite tier at 67.25, with his efficiency bolstered by a leadoff spot and a stolen base opportunity grade derived from Ginn\u2019s slow delivery to the plate.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision today involves the balancing act between pure power and efficiency. **Kyle Schwarber** (62.65) narrowly earns the UTIL nod over potential bench alternatives, as his current xwOBA of .392 suggests he is primed to capitalize on Ginn\u2019s middle-of-the-road 3.92 xERA. Conversely, **Caleb Durbin** rests at a marginal 41.71; while he is a primary target for our league-wide emphasis on stolen bases, his batting order position at #9 and a stiff matchup against a 3.02 xERA arm from Jax suppress his overall outlook. Looking at our depth chart, **Yordan Alvarez**, **Christian Yelich**, **Hyeseong Kim**, and **Kazuma Okamoto** are all unavailable due to scheduled off-days or IL designations, leaving limited room for rotation. As we move forward, we continue to monitor the development of **Colt Keith**, **Carson Benge**, **Gary Sanchez**, **Zac Veen**, **Luke Raley**, **Lars Nootbaar**, **Dillon Dingler**, **Josh Smith**, **Chase Meidroth**, **Luis Pena**, and **Pedro Ramirez** to ensure that if a starter stumbles or a platoon match swings unfavorably, our bench readiness remains high.",
